Pattern
Level	Holds	MTSV
file	an OTLP JSON Lines file, a TracesData or LogsData per line	one MTSV file
record	a span or a log record, grouped by resource and scope: its fields, its attributes, the 8 among them	the key record on every row of the 8; the record itself stays in the file
set	one of the 8, structured or a JSON string	Z: sheets of its own
item	a message, part, tool, memory record or document: a defined shape, or generic	Z: a sheet per shape; Y: a row per item
field	a simple value of an item	X: a column
nested	an item within an item	Z: a child sheet, joined by pointer
node	a value of no fixed shape	Z: a node sheet, a row per node
text	a string value	X: its cell
line	the text between line breaks	Y: a row of its lines sheet
character	HT, FF, or CR alone	not written
Rules
Id	Rule
file.1	The input is an OTLP JSON Lines file: UTF-8, one JSON value per line, each line a TracesData, MetricsData or LogsData in OTLP JSON encoding, one kind of data per file. The eight are recorded on spans and on events, so TracesData and LogsData carry them and MetricsData none.
file.2	The lines of a file are in no guaranteed order. They are read in the file's order, and nothing is sorted.
file.3	A receiver ignores fields with unknown names.
file.4	The output is one MTSV file: every sheet of the Sheets, in their order, each with its header, and no records where the input holds none.
record.1	A record is a span or a log record. Its address is a JSON Pointer to it in the file: the first token is the zero-based index of its line, the rest the pointer within that line's JSON value, as in /0/resourceSpans/0/scopeSpans/0/spans/0. Every record has an address and no two share one, so the address is the key a row of the 8 takes from its record; the record's other fields, its trace and span ids among them, stay with it in the file.
set.1	Each of the eight is found by its attribute key: gen_ai.system_instructions, gen_ai.tool.definitions, gen_ai.input.messages, gen_ai.output.messages, gen_ai.tool.call.arguments, gen_ai.tool.call.result, gen_ai.memory.records and gen_ai.retrieval.documents. On events it is structured; on spans it is structured, or a JSON string where structure is not supported. Both forms are read.
set.2	Each of the eight has sheets of its own. Input messages and output messages are kept apart, each with its own sheets.
item.1	Every row of a relation draws on the same domains, so a schema definition with fields of its own is a sheet of its own: each part type, each tool type and each record type. A part or tool belongs to the definition its type value names; one whose type names no definition is generic.
item.2	Every row of the 8 is keyed by record, the address of its record (record.1), and pointer, a JSON Pointer from the root of the attribute value to the row's value.
field.1	An enum (role, modality, finish_reason) holds a simple, atomic value, so it is a column.
nested.1	A nonsimple field is struck from its parent and becomes a child sheet that carries its parent's key; normalization applies because the nesting is a tree and no key is nonsimple. A child row's pointer extends its parent's pointer, so the parent's key is carried in it.
nested.2	The rows of a relation are unordered, while a JSON array is ordered and input messages are in the order sent. A JSON Pointer names an array element by its zero-based index, so order is kept in the pointer.
nested.3	Names are the sources' own: pointer for the JSON Pointer, record for the span or log record it addresses, type and value for a JSON value, and each schema's field names and definitions. A sheet's name is its parent's name, '.', and the field or definition it comes from; a definition is named by the type value its data carries.
node.1	A value of any shape is one sheet with a row per node: its pointer, its type (object, array, string, number, true, false or null) and its value. The value of a string is its text, of a number its text as written; an object, an array, true, false and null have an empty value. Rows are in the value's own order: a node before the nodes within it, members and elements as written.
node.2	Every object of the eight allows further properties. A property beyond its schema goes to its object's additionalProperties sheet, a node sheet named with the schemas' own keyword.
node.3	An implementation may limit the range and precision of numbers, so a number is written as the input writes it.
text.1	A field is text, and the optional fields of the eight default to null. Null, an absent field and the empty string are written as an empty field, and an empty field reads as null.
line.1	A line break (LF, or CRLF) means the next line, the same character MTSV uses for the next record. So a text value holding one is written as lines: its field is left empty, and each line is a record of its lines sheet, named its sheet's name, '.', and its column (value, for a node sheet), keyed by the pointer to the text value and line, the line's zero-based position. A line is the text between line breaks, so the lines joined with LF give the text back, a CRLF reading back as LF. Every text column and every node sheet's value has a lines sheet, which follows its sheet in the order of its columns.
character.1	A field that holds HT, LF, FF or CR cannot be represented, and a generator does not write it; the draft leaves its handling to the application, and here its field is left empty. A line break is carried as lines (line.1); HT, FF and a CR alone are not carried.
